WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.756
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:16.563
Unit eight: Futures Studies. Today you will forecast using the future perfect for long-range prediction.

4
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:19.458
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.714
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.981
Scenario planning.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.920
Scenario planning means imagining several possible futures to prepare for uncertainty.

8
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:36.919
Scenario planning helped the port prepare for sea-level rise.

9
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:40.297
Your turn. Say: Scenario planning.

10
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.518
Scenario planning.

11
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:46.888
Speculative futures.

12
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:52.704
Speculative futures means imaginative visions of what might happen, used to provoke debate.

13
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:56.965
Speculative futures often take the form of fiction or design.

14
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.493
Your turn. Say: Speculative futures.

15
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.880
Speculative futures.

16
00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:06.966
Emerging trend.

17
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.691
Emerging trend means a pattern of change that is beginning to appear.

18
00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:15.514
Remote work was an emerging trend long before the pandemic.

19
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:19.396
Your turn. Say: Emerging trend.

20
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:23.618
Emerging trend.

21
00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:25.373
Foresight.

22
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:31.297
Foresight means the ability to anticipate and prepare for future developments.

23
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.218
Foresight allowed the city to plan for an ageing population.

24
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:38.260
Your turn. Say: Foresight.

25
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:41.985
Foresight.

26
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.111
Systemic risk.

27
00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:49.717
Systemic risk means a danger that can cascade through a whole interconnected system.

28
00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:53.837
A single bank failure became a systemic risk to the economy.

29
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.288
Your turn. Say: Systemic risk.

30
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:01.645
Systemic risk.

31
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:03.826
Plausible future.

32
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:08.593
Plausible future means a future that could reasonably happen given what we know.

33
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:12.065
Planners explored three plausible futures for energy.

34
00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.778
Your turn. Say: Plausible future.

35
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:20.203
Plausible future.

36
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.683
Strategic anticipation.

37
00:02:23.683 --> 00:02:28.179
Strategic anticipation means preparing in advance for likely changes.

38
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:33.000
Strategic anticipation distinguished the firm from its slower rivals.

39
00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:36.861
Your turn. Say: Strategic anticipation.

40
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.164
Strategic anticipation.

41
00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:44.638
Long-term thinking.

42
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.111
Long-term thinking means making decisions with consequences decades ahead in mind.

43
00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:53.877
Long-term thinking is rare in election cycles.

44
00:02:54.483 --> 00:02:57.728
Your turn. Say: Long-term thinking.

45
00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:03.286
Long-term thinking.

46
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.446
Disruption risk.

47
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.574
Disruption risk means the danger that new technology will upend an industry.

48
00:03:11.183 --> 00:03:14.890
Taxi companies underestimated the disruption risk of ride-sharing.

49
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:18.449
Your turn. Say: Disruption risk.

50
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.742
Disruption risk.

51
00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:24.984
Resilience planning.

52
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:30.293
Resilience planning means preparing systems to absorb shocks and recover.

53
00:03:30.883 --> 00:03:34.206
Resilience planning included backup power for hospitals.

54
00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:37.806
Your turn. Say: Resilience planning.

55
00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:42.132
Resilience planning.

56
00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:46.305
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.564
Visal, how can anyone predict what the world will look like in fifty years?

58
00:03:52.650 --> 00:04:05.751
Futurists don't predict; they explore plausible futures. By 2075, foresight practices will have transformed policy-making, or so planners hope.

59
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.385
Hope isn't a method.

60
00:04:09.483 --> 00:04:20.135
True, which is why scenario planning is used. By the time the next decade ends, several emerging trends will have matured and others will have vanished.

61
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.016
So the point is preparation, not prophecy.

62
00:04:25.117 --> 00:04:34.921
Exactly. By the end of this century, societies that practised resilience planning will have survived shocks that destroyed those that did not.

63
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.370
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:38.217 --> 00:04:42.399
Grammar focus: Future perfect for long-range prediction.

65
00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:43.874
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:49.857
By 2075, foresight practices will have transformed policy-making.

67
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:54.537
By then, emerging trends will have matured into mainstream norms.

68
00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.317
By the century's end, resilient societies will have survived shocks that others could not.

69
00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:11.991
Future perfect = will have + past participle. It looks back from a future point: By 2075 we will have done X. Pair it with 'by' or 'by the time'.

70
00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:19.025
Be careful! Don't say: By 2075, foresight will transformed policy.

71
00:05:20.967 --> 00:05:25.973
Say: By 2075, foresight will have transformed policy.

72
00:05:26.273 --> 00:05:28.726
Future perfect: will have + p.p.

73
00:05:29.533 --> 00:05:32.209
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.082
Thinking Ahead.

75
00:05:34.382 --> 00:05:43.243
Futures studies does not claim to predict the future; it aims to map the range of plausible futures so that decisions can be made wisely under uncertainty.

76
00:05:44.233 --> 00:05:54.000
Scenario planning, developed by Shell in the 1970s, asks organisations to imagine several divergent worlds and to test their strategies against each of them.

77
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:02.329
By 2050, many analysts believe, automation and climate change will have reshaped labour markets and settlement patterns.

78
00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:12.055
Foresight also identifies systemic risks, the dangers that spread through interconnected systems, and encourages resilience planning before crises occur.

79
00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:18.596
Long-term thinking, though politically difficult, has repeatedly been shown to prevent costly surprises.

80
00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.702
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.616
By 2075, foresight blank policy-making.

82
00:06:28.916 --> 00:06:33.382
A, will transform. B, will have transformed. C, transformed.

83
00:06:36.933 --> 00:06:39.641
The answer is B: will have transformed.

84
00:06:40.783 --> 00:06:44.246
Imagining several possible futures to prepare is blank.

85
00:06:44.546 --> 00:06:49.000
A, scenario planning. B, deadlock. C, anchoring.

86
00:06:52.567 --> 00:06:54.891
The answer is A: scenario planning.

87
00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.687
A danger that cascades through a whole system is a blank risk.

88
00:06:59.987 --> 00:07:03.826
A, plausible. B, systemic. C, strategic.

89
00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:09.413
The answer is B: systemic.

90
00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.631
Choose the correct future perfect.

91
00:07:12.931 --> 00:07:22.447
A, By 2060 we will finish the project.. B, By 2060 we will have finish the project.. C, By 2060 we will have finished the project..

92
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.345
The answer is C: By 2060 we will have finished the project..

93
00:07:31.483 --> 00:07:34.284
According to the reading, futures studies aims to…

94
00:07:34.584 --> 00:07:40.223
A, predict exact events. B, map plausible futures. C, avoid planning.

95
00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:46.532
The answer is B: map plausible futures.

96
00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:50.897
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:55.688
What plausible future do you think is most likely in the next 50 years?

98
00:08:12.483 --> 00:08:13.972
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:08:14.272 --> 00:08:49.939
The most plausible future, in my view, is one shaped by climate adaptation and artificial intelligence. By 2075, cities will have redesigned coastlines and energy systems, and many routine jobs will have been automated. I can't be certain, of course, so I would prepare for several scenarios rather than one. Resilience matters more than prediction. Societies that invest in education and flexible institutions will have the best chance of thriving.

100
00:08:50.239 --> 00:08:52.111
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:53.017 --> 00:08:58.746
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and future perfect for long-range prediction.

102
00:08:59.046 --> 00:09:03.230
Today's value is foresight. Prepare today for the world of tomorrow.

103
00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:05.951
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

104
00:09:06.251 --> 00:09:08.822
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

